What the source build contains, beyond the release DLL
------------------------------------------------------

1. A, source form -- no second 81-slot ceiling

   The release DLL checked a weapon give twice: the pickup gate (RVA 0x567a0) counted the
   bonus ts_setuserslots stores, then the inventory add routine (RVA 0x622d0) refused past a
   fixed 81 carried-weight. Patch A nop'd the second check. In the source there is no second
   check to remove: CTSGun::TakeWeapon (dlls\ts_gun.cpp) has no weight test at all, and the
   only gate is TSPlayerFreeSlots (dlls\ts_gun.cpp; RVA 0x61460 in the release), which is
   81 - carried - flag(20) - kevlar(10) + the plugin's bonus. A player the plugin never gave
   room is therefore still held to 81 exactly as before.

2. B, source form -- the powerup grant, for ts_matchmode only

   CTSPowerUp::Spawn (dlls\ts_powerup.cpp) arms the powerup's touch handler at spawn, before
   the materialize think, when spawnflags has bit 30 (SF_TSM_PWUP_GRANT) and the cvar
   tsm_enabled exists and is non-zero. Every other powerup still gets Touch = NULL until it
   lands with nopowerup off, so nopowerup 1 still blocks every other powerup, and every server
   without ts_matchmode. This is the source-level replacement for patch B's 7-byte hook and
   its .h9pwup stub.

3. The disarm fix

   A disarm calls CBasePlayer::DropWeapon(-2, damage); past the melee-pressure threshold it
   calls CTSGun::DropWeapon(type, fThrow = false). That used to clear the item and leave the
   player holding a weapon with nothing owned behind it -- still deployed, still firable.
   CTSGun::DropWeapon now calls HolsterWeapon() when the dropped type is the active one and
   fThrow is false, so the player goes to bare hands (viewmodel, weaponmodel, active pointers,
   m_weaponType -> TSW_MELEE, kung-fu draw). A real throw (the drop key, type -1) still calls
   GetBetterWeapon() and auto-selects the next-best weapon, as before.

   Faithful to TS: a normal weapon is removed outright; stackable spares (knife, sealknife,
   m61) and a single whose akimbo is also owned keep the remainder, and those paths already
   holstered.

4. Interface 5/6 alias

   The source defines RETS_INTERFACE_VERSION 6 and appends PowerUpTouch after every v5 slot.
   The deployed reTSX module (tsx_amxx.dll) still calls ReTS_GetInterface(5) and never reads
   the table's version field, so against a source build every ReTS native returned null
   (ts_setusercash, weaponrestriction, ...). dlls\rets_api.cpp now builds two tables:
   BuildInterface(6) for callers that ask for 6, and BuildInterface(5) -- the same 0xD0-byte
   layout with version = 5 -- for callers that ask for 5. Both are static, filled at load.
   Interface 5 is a strict prefix of 6, so a v5 caller reading any v5 slot gets the right
   function; it never reads the appended PowerUpTouch.

5. The two upstream commits of 2026-10-29

   e82e40282a4d "Moving fire penalises only the M60 and the Barrett, as TS 3.0 does"
     dlls\ts_gun.cpp, CTSGun::StandardFire: the x5 moving growth is now the M60's alone
     (over 175 u/s), and the Barrett sets the spread accumulator to 0.3 over 35 u/s. Every
     other gun keeps its standing growth however fast the player moves.

   bd3f6c3c1aa3 "Gunshot and ricochet pitch unclamped, as TS 3.0 plays them"
     cl_dll\ev_ts.cpp: the 255/1 clamp is gone from the gunshot, linked-fire and ricochet
     pitches. pitch = 100 + 300 * slow, 400 at normal speed; neither GoldSrc nor Xash caps
     pitch, so normal fire plays at 4x again instead of 2.55x.


6. Built for 128 player slots (docs\PLAYER_LIMIT_128.txt)

   dlls\cdll_dll.h: MAX_PLAYERS 128, shared with the client. Every table sized by it follows
   (the voice ban and game-rules masks, pm_shared's stuck tables), and:
   - game_shared\bitvec.h: CBitVec's dword count came out as 1 for any size (an operator
     precedence slip in NUM_DWORDS); it rounds NUM_BITS up to whole dwords. Every bit vector
     past 32 bits depends on it.
   - VoiceMask carries a [rules, ban] dword pair per 32 slots in use, sized once per process at
     its first registration: 8 bytes on a server of 32 or fewer.
   - CBasePlayer::m_hSlowPlayers (32 entries, at the original's offset 0x188) holds slots 1 to 32
     of the slow-motion cache; slots 33 and up are in m_hSlowPlayersHigh, a new member at the end
     of CBasePlayer. SlowMotionFlow used to write up to maxplayers entries into the 32: memory
     past the table on a server of more than 32 slots.
   - CTSSlowMotion, CTSSlowMotionPoint and CTSTeamEscape keep their 33-entry tables in place and
     take entindex 33 and up in new members at their class ends. Flying bullets' hit boxes
     (vecBoxes) and The One's draw (eligible) cover every slot.
   - trigger_hurt: slots 1 to 32 keep their "hurt this tick" bit in pev->impulse; slots past 32
     have theirs in CTriggerHurt::m_touchedHigh.
   - AddToFullPack sends entities up to the engine's own limit on Xash (gpGlobals->maxEntities,
     gameinfo.txt's max_edicts) and up to 2047 on GoldSrc (11-bit entity numbers); Xash is told
     apart by its host_ver cvar.
   No member moves: every new member is at the end of its class. On a server of 32 slots or fewer
   the game plays and sends what it did before.

7. A shot fired lying down keeps its pose

   dlls\ts_animation.cpp, CTSAnimation::Animate, case 3 -- the lying pose HandleSlide asks for
   every frame while a player is down after a dive: it records m_iLastActivity = 3 also when it
   leaves a shot's slide_<dir>_<grip>_shoot pose playing, as TS 3.0 does (its case 3 breaks out
   of the switch, and the code after the switch records the activity either way; Animate,
   ts_i386.so 0x5b470). From the next frame Region B's case 3 runs instead, which re-poses only
   when the aim swaps side, so a player firing while prone stays in the firing pose. Before,
   the lying pose came back each time a shot's sequence ended, and steady fire bobbed the body
   up and down against the floor. Server-side only: the client does not compile
   ts_animation.cpp.

8. Team names up to 31 characters

   dlls\player.h, dlls\teamplay_gamerules.h, dlls\teamplay_gamerules.cpp: a player's team name
   (CBasePlayer::m_szTeamName, TEAM_NAME_LENGTH) and the teamplay rules' list of team names
   (MAX_TEAMNAME_LENGTH) hold 32 bytes, as TSGameRules' own m_teamNames already did, so a name such
   as "Middle Sea Republic" reaches TeamInfo, the scoreboard and the HUD whole and as written; the
   original's 16 cut a name at 15 characters. m_szTeamName is now CBasePlayer's last member and its
   original 16-byte slot stays where it was, unused (m_szTeamNameAtOrig), so no other member moves.
   Every copy into these is cut to fit and ended, a client's "team" key included. The client keeps
   a team name in 32 bytes too (cl_dll\global_consts.h MAX_TEAM_NAME, TeamInfo's copy ended), in
   the client.dll built 2026-11-02. A name of 15 characters or fewer plays as before.

